AVM: with host loopback disabled, a guest can't name the host's loopback itself

disable_host_loopback (which AVM's QEMU patch no-host-loopback turns on)
refuses only a destination that is the gateway alias, 10.0.2.2, or the
guest's IPv6 prefix. A destination the guest writes as 127.0.0.1, any
other 127/8 address, 0.0.0.0 or ::1 is outside the virtual network, so it
was passed on as it was, and libslirp connected to it on the host: the
host's loopback after all. A guest with root needs only to put such a
packet on its network card (2026-11-10, AVM's audit of that day #1).
With the switch on, these are refused too, with IPv4-mapped and
IPv4-compatible IPv6 addresses, which reach the host's IPv4 stack from an
IPv6 socket, and a ping of any of them is dropped, not passed on.
Applies to libslirp 4.9.1 after UTM's patch.

--- a/src/socket.c
+++ b/src/socket.c
@@ -980,6 +980,15 @@
         sin->sin_addr = loopback_addr;
     }
 
+    /* AVM: the host's own loopback and unspecified addresses, named by
+     * the guest itself, are the host's loopback too. */
+    if (s->disable_host_loopback) {
+        uint32_t dst = ntohl(so->so_faddr.s_addr);
+        if ((dst >> 24) == 127 || (dst >> 24) == 0) {
+            return false;
+        }
+    }
+
     return true;
 }
 
@@ -1003,6 +1012,16 @@
         sin->sin6_addr = in6addr_loopback;
     }
 
+    /* AVM: as for IPv4. An IPv4-mapped address reaches the host's IPv4
+     * stack from an IPv6 socket, 127.0.0.1 included. */
+    if (s->disable_host_loopback &&
+        (IN6_IS_ADDR_LOOPBACK(&so->so_faddr6) ||
+         IN6_IS_ADDR_UNSPECIFIED(&so->so_faddr6) ||
+         IN6_IS_ADDR_V4MAPPED(&so->so_faddr6) ||
+         IN6_IS_ADDR_V4COMPAT(&so->so_faddr6))) {
+        return false;
+    }
+
     return true;
 }
 
